  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nThe long term period will start off on a largely dry note Sunday, \nwith more isolated showers lingering over the area as a slow-moving \nmid-level trough sweeps through the area. Persistent southeasterly \nflow will help high temperatures warm to the middle to upper 60s.\n\nThe main focus for the long-term continues to be on a large system \nslated to translate through the central CONUS for Monday through \nWednesday morning, including Election Day. The culprit for this \nactive period is an amplified upper-level trough and an associated \nsurface low that's progged to translate through the Midwest. GEFS \nand EC ensemble guidance suggests a tropical moisture connection \nwith this system, drawing some impressive environmental moisture \ninto the area. In fact, the LREF ensemble mean for Pwats are \nhovering around 1.4 to 1.6\" Monday night and Tuesday morning. If \nthese values come to fruition during this time of year, these Pwat \nvalues would be well over the daily maximum for Pwats per the SPC \nsounding climatology for November 3rd. This is also backed up by the \nNAEFS ensemble climatological percentile values, suggesting these \nPwats would be near the maximum of the climatology for early \nNovember. With this said, there still remains some uncertainty on \nthe exact track of the surface low, given continued spread of the \nlow tracks per the EC and GEFS ensembles. Where this surface low \ngoes will dictate where the axis of the heaviest rainfall and \nstrongest winds will be. The latest runs of these ensembles are \nstarting to show some better consensus of the low tracks, mainly \nover western Iowa into Minnesota, indicating our region would be in \nthe warm sector of the system. This would suggest rainy conditions \nfor Election Day, but not as windy as it would be if we were on the \nbackside of the surface low. With that said, winds with this system \ngenerally look to be around 20-30 mph, so nothing too impactful. For \nareas that receive appreciable rainfall, this could make for some \ngloomy weather conditions on Election Day. In terms of rainfall \namounts, there appears to be a consistent signal, but due to the \nuncertain low track, the location of the axis of rainfall remains \nuncertain. However, the latest NBM exceedance probabilities of 1\" of \ntotal rain or more during this entire period is around 60-90% for \nmost locations, with 2\" probabilities around 20-60%. Both of these \nare maximized over eastern Iowa.",
